# Line endings, stated per file type rather than globally.
#
# Every source blob here is already LF; what was missing was anything saying
# so. With core.autocrlf=true the working copy gets CRLF on checkout even
# though the stored bytes are LF, which is why `git add` printed "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" on every file — and it is what makes byte-for-byte
# comparison with the Packers repo impossible while the two share code.
#
# There is deliberately no `* text=auto` line. Naming the source extensions
# means nothing under data/ is matched by an eol rule by accident — the CSV
# rule below is then the only thing that speaks for 458MB of Retrosheet data,
# rather than a walk-back of something broader.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf

# Retrosheet ships its files with CRLF and they are consumed byte-for-byte —
# CurrentNames.csv carries 126 CRs, allplayers.csv over 2,000. Others in the
# same directory are LF. Both are correct: whatever arrived is what is stored.
#
# -text, not the !text this line used to carry. !text means UNSPECIFIED, which
# falls back to core.autocrlf — true on this machine — and strips the CRs on
# the next add. That was latent rather than harmless: `git add --renormalize`
# under !text rewrote seven of these files and took CurrentNames.csv from 126
# CRs to none. -text is the explicit "do no conversion" that was meant.
#
# It only became a no-op after four files were restored on disk: an earlier
# checkout under the old rule had left ballparks, batting, fielding and
# managers with CRLF working copies over LF blobs, which `git status` hid by
# normalising before comparing. Deleting and re-checking-out those four is what
# made disk and blob agree.
#
# This line also unsets `filter`, which is why the plays rules below have to
# re-establish LFS afterwards. Fragile, but it is the fragility that already
# existed rather than any this change introduces.
*.csv -text !filter !merge !diff

# The play-by-play file is 387MB and lives in Git LFS. Re-stated after the CSV
# exemption above, which would otherwise strip the filter off it.
*.lfs.csv filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/plays.csv filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
data/plays.lfs.csv filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Compressed artifacts are binary and must never be touched. Without this they
# resolve to "unspecified", which falls back to core.autocrlf — and a single CR
# rewritten inside a brotli stream makes the file undecodable, which the server
# would report as a corrupt artifact and quietly fall back to a five-second CSV
# boot for.
*.br binary
*.ndjson.br binary

# Fonts and images are binary. text=auto would detect that on its own, but
# saying it outright means they can never be corrupted by a rule added above.
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
